      "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n#define TILE_SIZE 128 // inner-loop tile size for cache locality\n\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact among all pairs of bodies in \n * the system. Uses Structure-of-Arrays layout and tiling.\n */\nvoid body_force(float * restrict x, float * restrict y, float * restrict z,\n                float * restrict vx, float * restrict vy, float * restrict vz,\n                float dt, int n) {\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float fx = 0.0f;\n    float fy = 0.0f;\n    float fz = 0.0f;\n\n    const float xi = x[i];\n    const float yi = y[i];\n    const float zi = z[i];\n\n    // Tile the inner loop to improve cache reuse\n    for (int jj = 0; jj < n; jj += TILE_SIZE) {\n      int end = jj + TILE_SIZE;\n      if (end > n) end = n;\n\n      for (int j = jj; j < end; ++j) {\n        float dx = x[j] - xi;\n        float dy = y[j] - yi;\n        float dz = z[j] - zi;\n        float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n        float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n        float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n        fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n        fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n        fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n      }\n    }\n\n    vx[i] += dt*fx;\n    vy[i] += dt*fy;\n    vz[i] += dt*fz;\n  }\n}\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initialized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n\n  Body *tmp = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  // Convert from AoS (Body array) to SoA (separate float arrays)\n  float *x  = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *y  = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *z  = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vx = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vy = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  float *vz = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    x[i]  = tmp[i].x;\n    y[i]  = tmp[i].y;\n    z[i]  = tmp[i].z;\n    vx[i] = tmp[i].vx;\n    vy[i] = tmp[i].vy;\n    vz[i] = tmp[i].vz;\n  }\n  free(tmp);\n\n  /*\n   * At each simulation iteration, interbody forces are computed,\n   * and bodies' positions are integrated.\n   */\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    body_force(x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, DT, nbodies);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n      x[i] += vx[i] * DT;\n      y[i] += vy[i] * DT;\n      z[i] += vz[i] * DT;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Convert back from SoA to AoS for output\n  Body *out = (Body *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n    out[i].x  = x[i];\n    out[i].y  = y[i];\n    out[i].z  = z[i];\n    out[i].vx = vx[i];\n    out[i].vy = vy[i];\n    out[i].vz = vz[i];\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, out);\n\n  free(out);\n  free(x); free(y); free(z); free(vx); free(vy); free(vz);\n\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n",
      "path": "src/nbody.c"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Converting the data layout from Array-of-Structures (AoS) to Structure-of-Arrays (SoA) improves spatial locality when iterating over body coordinates in the inner loop, enabling better cache utilization and auto-vectorization. Adding inner-loop tiling with a tile size of 128 further enhances L1 cache reuse by loading a block of bodies once per outer iteration. Restrict qualifiers on pointer arguments help the compiler eliminate aliasing assumptions and generate more efficient code. These transformations preserve the exact floating-point operation order (i outer, j inner sequential) so that output matches the reference bit-for-bit.",
  "notes": "This transformation is similar to a previous correct attempt but uses restrict pointers for additional optimization potential. The tile size of 128 was chosen empirically; it fits within typical L1 cache sizes and avoids excessive loop overhead. Self-interaction (i==j) is retained because its contribution is zero, ensuring identical floating-point accumulation order.",
